curated-local-cadence-memory:
### Goal
Build a 2048 × 128 memory (11-bit address, 128-bit data) from 1024 × 64 blocks (10-bit address, 64-bit data, active-low CE and WE).

### Sizing
Depth 2048 / 1024 = 2 banks. Width 128 / 64 = 2 blocks per bank. Total 4 blocks.

### Connections
- Address: Addr[9:0] goes to the address pins of all four blocks; Addr[10] selects the bank.
- Data in: bits [127:64] go to the "high" block of each bank, bits [63:0] to the "low" block.
- Chip enable (active low): nCE_bank0 = ~(Enable & ~Addr[10]); nCE_bank1 = ~(Enable & Addr[10]). Both blocks of a bank share the bank CE.
- Write enable (active low): nWE = ~WriteRead (write = 1 → nWE = 0), shared by all blocks.
- Data out: Dout[127:64] = Addr[10] ? bank1_high : bank0_high; Dout[63:0] = Addr[10] ? bank1_low : bank0_low. If the memory read is synchronous, register Addr[10] with the clock so the select aligns with the data.

### Verilog sketch
```verilog
wire sel1 = en &  a[10];
wire sel0 = en & ~a[10];
mem1024x64 b0h(.clk(clk), .addr(a[9:0]), .nce(~sel0), .nwe(~wr), .din(din[127:64]), .dout(d0h));
mem1024x64 b0l(.clk(clk), .addr(a[9:0]), .nce(~sel0), .nwe(~wr), .din(din[63:0]),   .dout(d0l));
mem1024x64 b1h(.clk(clk), .addr(a[9:0]), .nce(~sel1), .nwe(~wr), .din(din[127:64]), .dout(d1h));
mem1024x64 b1l(.clk(clk), .addr(a[9:0]), .nce(~sel1), .nwe(~wr), .din(din[63:0]),   .dout(d1l));
reg a10_q; always @(posedge clk) a10_q <= a[10];
assign dout = a10_q ? {d1h, d1l} : {d0h, d0l};
```

### Trade-offs and follow-ups
- Why register Addr[10]? With a one-cycle read latency the output multiplexer select must correspond to the address of the data being output.
- Power: only the selected bank is enabled.
- What if the data bus is bidirectional? Add tri-state or separate din/dout and gate dout with the read enable.
